2186045585 true] [17592186397842 1069 "q-135105" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397842 1071 "An object is projected vertically down from a height of $0.6\\,\\text{km}$ and hits the ground in $6\\,\\text{s}.$ What was the initial velocity of the object?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397842 1072 #uuid "04947b95-8a1f-567f-948f-2de5de86130e" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397842 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The object falls for $t=6\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the object falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =0.6\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =0.6\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =600\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (600) & =u(6)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(6)^{2} \\\\ 600 & =6u+176.4 \\\\ 423.6 & =6u \\\\ u & =70.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the object was $u=70.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397844 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397845 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397846 131941