^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A+4I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A+4I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A+4I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A+4I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A+4I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A+4I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A+4I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A+4I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A+4I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A+4I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397879 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397880 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397881 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397882 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397883 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397878 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 131941395334